    "bytes": 1587333,
    "pages": 332,
    "note": "The publisher's own current revision. The prose carries a clean text layer; the large opcode map tables do not, and reading them from the extracted text produces characters that are not in the printed tables. Every figure in this file was taken either from the prose or from a per-instruction timing table, and the three rows where the printed M Cycles column disagrees with its own breakdown were checked against the rendered page image one by one."
  },
  "facts": {
    "machineCycleShapes": {
      "note": "What each kind of machine cycle looks like on the bus, from the Timing chapter. These are the shapes a cycle-by-cycle model has to produce; the per-instruction table below says how many of each an instruction performs.",
      "opcodeFetch": {
        "tStates": 4,
        "quote": "The first machine cycle of any instruction is a fetch cycle that is four, five, or six T cycles long (unless lengthened by the WAIT signal, which is described in the next section).",
        "addressInT1T2": "programCounter",
        "addressInT3T4": "refresh",
        "refreshQuote": "During T3 and T4, the lower seven bits of the address bus contain a memory refresh address and the RFSH signal becomes active, indicating that a refresh read of all dynamic memories must be performed. To prevent data from different memory segments from being gated onto the data bus, an RD signal is not generated during this refresh period.",
        "dataSampledQuote": "The CPU samples the data from the memory space on the data bus with the rising edge of the clock of state T3, and this same edge is used by the CPU to turn off the RD and MREQ signals.",
        "manualPage": 8
      },
      "memoryReadOrWrite": {
        "tStates": 3,
        "quote": "Figure 6 shows the timing of memory read or write cycles other than an op code fetch cycle. These cycles are generally three clock periods long unless wait states are requested by memory through the WAIT signal.",
        "manualPage": 9
      },
      "inputOrOutput": {
        "tStates": 4,
        "automaticWaitStates": 1,
        "quote": "During I/O operations, a single wait state is automatically inserted. The reason for this single wait state insertion is that during I/O operations, the period from when the IORQ signal goes active until the CPU must sample the WAIT line is short.",
        "why": "This is why an I/O machine cycle is four T states where a memory access is three. The wait is not requested by the device and cannot be declined.",
        "manualPage": 10
      },
      "interruptAcknowledge": {
        "tStates": 6,
        "automaticWaitStates": 2,
        "quote": "When the signal is accepted, a special M1 cycle is generated. During this special M1 cycle, the IORQ signal becomes active (instead of the normal MREQ) to indicate that the interrupting device can place an 8-bit vector on the data bus. Two wait states are automatically added to this cycle.",
        "note": "Six T states is the four of a normal opcode fetch plus the two the manual says are added. The manual states the addition rather than printing the total.",
        "inferred": "tStates",
        "manualPage": 12
      },
      "nonmaskableInterrupt": {
        "quote": "The CPU response to a nonmaskable interrupt is similar to a normal memory read operation. The only difference is that the contents of the data bus are ignored while the processor automatically stores the Program Counter in the external stack and jumps to address 0066h.",
        "manualPage": 13
      },
      "halt": {
        "quote": "Each cycle in the HALT state is a normal M1 (fetch) cycle except that the data received from the memory is ignored and an NOP instruction is forced internally to the CPU.",
        "why": "A halted part is not idle on the bus. It keeps fetching so that dynamic memory keeps being refreshed, which is why a model that stops issuing cycles while halted is not modelling this part.",
        "manualPage": 14
      }
    },
    "pins": {
      "note": "Only the pins a bus recording can observe. The recording names four of them.",
      "m1": {
        "quote": "M1. Machine Cycle One (output, active Low). M1, together with MREQ, indicates that the current machine cycle is the op code fetch cycle of an instruction execution. M1, when operating together with IORQ, indicates an interrupt acknowledge cycle."
      },
      "mreq": {
        "quote": "MREQ. Memory Request (output, active Low, tristate). MREQ indicates that the address bus holds a valid address for a memory read or a memory write operation."
      },
      "iorq": {
        "quote": "IORQ. Input/Output Request (output, active Low, tristate). IORQ indicates that the lower half of the address bus holds a valid I/O address for an I/O read or write operation."
      },
      "rd": {
        "quote": "RD. Read (output, active Low, tristate). RD indicates that the CPU wants to read data from memory or an I/O device."
      },
      "wr": {
        "quote": "WR. Write (output, active Low, tristate). WR indicates that the CPU data bus contains valid data to be stored at the addressed memory or I/O location."
      },
      "rfsh": {
        "quote": "RFSH. Refresh (output, active Low). RFSH, together with MREQ, indicates that the lower seven bits of the system's address bus can be used as a refresh address to the system's dynamic memories."
      }
    },
    "reset": {
      "quote": "RESET. Reset (input, active Low). RESET initializes the CPU as follows: it resets the interrupt enable flip-flop, clears the Program Counter and registers I and R, and sets the interrupt status to Mode 0. During reset time, the address and data bus enter a high-impedance state, and all control output signals enter an inactive state. RESET must be active for a minimum of three full clock cycles before a reset operation is complete.",
      "clears": ["pc", "i", "r"],
      "interruptMode": 0,
      "clearsInterruptEnable": true,
      "leavesEverythingElse": "The manual names what reset defines. Every other register keeps what it held, which is why this model seeds them rather than clearing them.",
      "minimumClockCycles": 3,
      "manualPage": 6
    },
    "flagRegister": {
      "quote": "Table 21. Flag Register Bit Positions. Bit Position 7 S, 6 Z, 5 X, 4 H, 3 X, 2 P/V, 1 N, 0 C",
      "bits": {
        "c": 0,
        "n": 1,
        "pv": 2,
        "x": 3,
        "h": 4,
        "y": 5,
        "z": 6,
        "s": 7
      },
      "unusedBitsQuote": "Each of these two Flag registers contains 6 bits of status information that are set or cleared by CPU operations; bits 3 and 5 are not used.",
      "note": "The manual calls bits 3 and 5 not used and gives them no behaviour. The part drives them anyway, and what it drives them with is settled by the recording rather than by this file. That is an absence in the document, not a contradiction of it.",
      "manualPage": 65
    },
    "interruptModes": {
      "count": 3,
      "quote": "The Z80 CPU contains two interrupt inputs: a software maskable interrupt (INT) and a nonmaskable interrupt (NMI).",
      "nmiVector": 102,
      "nmiVectorQuote": "NMI is always recognized at the end of the current instruction, independent of the status of the interrupt enable flip-flop, and automatically forces the CPU to restart at location 0066h."
    },
